# GitHub workflow

## Branches

- **Always create a branch** for any feature, fix, or test task. One per logical
  unit of work, like the PR. Never commit directly to `main`.
- **Naming (MVDS convention):** `<type>/<short-descriptor>` in kebab-case, where
  type is `feat` / `fix` / `docs` / `chore`. The descriptor reflects the actual
  work, not the chat title (e.g. `feat/figma-spatial-primitives`, not
  `claude/review-ad-campaign-abc123`). Carry the name through to the PR title.

## Pull requests

- **Always open a PR** when the work is complete, via the GitHub CLI
  (`gh pr create`). Open as a **draft** while iterating; flag it **ready for
  review** once all checks come back green. The founder always gives final
  approval.
- **One PR per logical unit of work.** Don't bundle unrelated changes.
- **Never have two PRs open for the same work item.** If a PR exists for the
  current task, push follow-up commits to that branch and note them in the PR
  description; keep iterating on the existing PR until all checks pass.
- **Never merge or approve a PR.** The founder reviews and merges. Agents must
  not run `gh pr merge` or approve reviews.
- **PR description** must include: (a) a list of **all** code changes in the PR,
  and (b) the **founder's original intent** in the founder's own framing — the
  *why*, not just the *what*. Don't include a test plan unless it's an actual
  automated test that was implemented.
- **Copilot review comments:** surface each one to the founder before acting;
  verify what Copilot actually landed against the real branch tip (see
  AGENTS.md step 5).

## After merge

- **Clean up what shipped:** prune remote-tracking refs, remove the session
  worktree, delete the local branch. The `.githooks/post-merge` hook automates
  this on every `git pull` into main (safe forms only — see AGENTS.md step 7).

## Commits

- Conventional-commit style matching the branch type: `feat(scope): …`,
  `fix(scope): …`, `docs(scope): …`, `chore(scope): …`.
